# Push and Deploy

After finishing any implementation or meaningful code change in this project, **always commit and push to deploy** unless the user says not to.

Also run this workflow when the user asks to push, deploy, ship, or publish.

## Project targets

- **Git remote:** `origin` → `https://github.com/Bidex20256/flowbase-.git`
- **Branch:** `main` (tracks `origin/main`)
- **Live site:** https://flowbase-tau.vercel.app
- **Deploy path:** Push to `main` → Vercel auto-deploys from GitHub

## When to push

- After completing a feature, fix, polish, or responsive/design change
- When the user says push / deploy / ship / publish
- Do **not** leave finished work unpushed

## Workflow

1. Run in parallel: `git status`, `git diff` (staged + unstaged), `git log -5 --oneline`
2. If there are changes:
   - Stage relevant files (include `.cursor/rules` when updated; never `.env` or secrets)
   - Commit with a concise message (PowerShell here-string / HEREDOC)
3. `git push origin main`
4. Confirm remote is still `Bidex20256/flowbase-`
5. Tell the user:
   - GitHub: https://github.com/Bidex20256/flowbase-
   - Live: https://flowbase-tau.vercel.app
   - Vercel may take ~30–60s to update

## Rules

- Do **not** force-push to `main` unless explicitly requested
- Do **not** skip git hooks
- Do **not** change `git config`
- Do **not** create a different remote or repo unless asked
- Prefer `gh` for PRs/issues; use `git push` for deploy
- Static HTML/CSS/JS — no build step for Vercel
